2. Research Questions and Falsifiable Hypotheses

RQ1: How do individual‑level cultural values moderate the speed, stability, and calibration of trust toward service robots?

RQ2: Does an exogenous regulatory signal (EU AI Act certification) increase trust and reduce anxiety in Paris and Rome, and is this effect modulated by the timeline uncertainty of the regulation’s implementation?

RQ3: How does robot‑induced autonomy degradation interact with cultural values and regulatory protection to produce psychological and economic costs?

RQ4: Can a formally specified Socratic debiasing intervention break pathological trust lock‑in and prevent dependency in ageing populations?

RQ5: Do city‑sector‑specific labour‑market dynamics diverge in the employment effects of robotisation, mediated by labour‑cost levels and employment‑protection legislation?

Hypothesis 1 (Trust trajectory). In agents with high Security values (a proxy for uncertainty avoidance), trust will increase more slowly but remain more stable under repeated robot interaction; in low‑Security agents, trust will rise and fall rapidly.

Hypothesis 2 (Regulatory premium with timing uncertainty). The EU AI Act certification boolean will increase trust significantly in Paris and Rome only when the regulation is effectively in force; during the deferral period (pre‑2027), its effect will be absent, creating a trust‑fragility window.

Hypothesis 3 (Autonomy degradation). Doubling robot density in an agent’s occupation sector reduces perceived autonomy by 1 %, with the effect amplified by high Security and mitigated by the presence of the EU AI Act’s risk‑assessment obligation.

Hypothesis 4 (Socratic intervention). In Rome and Paris, older agents receiving Socratic triggers when trust exceeds a threshold will show a 20‑percentage‑point lower dependency ratio at year 10 compared to controls.

Hypothesis 5 (Employment pathways). In moderate‑labour‑cost sectors in Paris and Rome, robot exposure lowers job separation rates (complementarity); in New York’s high‑flexibility, routine‑service sectors, robot exposure increases separations.

