3. Literature and Theoretical Foundations

3.1 Culture at the Individual Level: Schwartz Values

The proposal operationalises culture via Schwartz’s ten basic values (Self‑Direction, Security, Conformity, Tradition, etc.). The Security value is particularly relevant: it indexes a person’s need for safety, stability, and rule‑governed order—conceptually identical to uncertainty avoidance but measured at the individual level. Recent work has begun to apply Schwartz values to HRI design. A 2025 Korean study mapped Schwartz’s basic individual values to service scenarios for personal emotional intelligence robots, demonstrating the feasibility of such an individual‑level approach in an HRI context. A 2025 PhD thesis at Swansea University built a cross‑cultural trust‑measurement framework integrating cultural factors and mathematical modeling, showing that trust factors vary significantly across cultures and HRI scenarios. However, a 2025 scoping review of trust and cultural dimensions in HRI found only seven studies meeting inclusion criteria, none involving physical robots, and none using individual‑level value scales. This confirms the originality of using Schwartz microdata to initialise agents.

The European Social Survey (Rounds 8–10) includes the 21‑item Portrait Values Questionnaire for France and Italy; the World Values Survey Wave 7 provides the same for the United States. A 2025 validation study confirms that indexes for eight of the ten Schwartz values can be reliably constructed from the Rokeach Value Survey. As a fallback for the USA, the General Social Survey contains comparable value measures and will be used if WVS approval is delayed.

3.2 Computational Trust: The Guo & Yang Beta‑Distribution Model

The simulation adopts the Bayesian trust model of Guo & Yang (2020). Trust is modelled as a Beta random variable:

\theta_{t} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_t, \beta_t)

where α_t and β_t are updated after each interaction. The mean trust at time t is α_t/(α_t+β_t). The model achieved an RMSE of 0.072 in a collaborative assembly task and identified three dynamic trust types: Bayesian decision maker, oscillator, and disbeliever. A 2025 extension, the Trustworthy Influence Protocol (TIP), extends the Beta model to multi‑human multi‑robot teams with direct and indirect trust propagation, achieving lower RMSE than direct‑only models. The present study uses the simpler Guo & Yang foundation because of its comparability across thousands of agents, but TIP is acknowledged as a state‑of‑the‑art alternative that will be employed in a robustness analysis.

This base model is extended by: (a) modulating the initial prior with the agent’s Security value; (b) adding a regulatory trust premium when the robot is certified under the EU AI Act; (c) subtracting an anxiety penalty derived from the Robot Anxiety Scale and amplified by high Security; and (d) introducing an automation‑bias term that can cause a failure to be miscoded as a success for bias‑prone agents.

3.3 Robot Anxiety, Fear Taxonomies, and the Uncanny Valley

A 2025 scoping review on fear of robots among older adults identified seven fear categories: privacy, reliability, emotional discomfort, usability, dependence, unfamiliarity, and the Uncanny Valley. This taxonomy is adopted directly. Each city is assigned a primary fear driver informed by empirical surveys: Paris (privacy), Rome (emotional/ethical discomfort), New York (Uncanny Valley/dependence). The validated Robot Anxiety Scale (RAS) provides a baseline anxiety score for each agent, which is updated by exposure frequency, the presence of certification, and decreasing unfamiliarity.

Stock‑Homburg & Nguyen (2023, updated 2026) experimentally demonstrated automation bias in HRI with social robots, showing that bias is enhanced by robot competence, is stronger for emotional than analytical tasks, and is surprisingly negatively related to robot reliability. This finding justifies a modelling rule in which the automation‑bias term is stronger for social/emotional robot types and is triggered even by highly reliable (but over‑trusted) robots. A 2025 study with older adults further showed that their trust is resilient to errors, remaining high regardless of robot advice quality, which underscores the need for a formal debiasing intervention.

3.4 Media Framing and Public Perception

Brauner et al. (2026) conducted a pre‑registered experiment (N = 1 150) on the effects of positive versus negative media framing on perceived risk and benefit in human–robot collaboration. Key results: negative framing produced a strong interaction effect (risk × benefit β = −0.32), while positive framing supported additive evaluations. Model fit was R² = 0.715 (positive frame) and 0.583 (negative frame). The risk perception shift under negative framing was −0.69 on the standardised scale. These effect sizes are used directly to parameterise the media‑event shocks in the simulation. A companion study in Scientific Reports (2026) on the stability of moral attributions to humanoid robots under framing provides boundary conditions for when media effects persist or decay.

3.5 Regulatory Environment

The EU AI Act entered into force on 1 August 2024. As of April 2026, the Council and Parliament have converged on deferring high‑risk obligations for stand‑alone Annex III systems from 2 August 2026 to 2 December 2027, and for Annex I embedded systems to 2 August 2028. The original deadline remains in law unless the trilogue reaches final agreement. Italy enacted Law No. 132/2025, effective from October 2025, introducing new criminal penalties for malicious deepfakes (1–5 years’ imprisonment under Art. 612‑quater c.p.) and enhanced penalties when AI is used to commit crimes (Art. 61 n.11‑decies c.p.). The model treats EU AI Act certification status as a time‑varying state: “pending” (pre‑certification), “active” (post‑certification), or “deferred”, with transition dates drawn from a Monte Carlo distribution reflecting the current policy uncertainty. Italy’s national law adds a constant policy trust premium in the Rome scenario for privacy‑related robot interactions.

3.6 Cross‑Cultural Empirical Benchmarks

Fracasso et al. (2022) conducted a cross‑national study with 197 older adults (Italy: M = 67.87 years; Germany: M = 62.15 years) on social assistive robot acceptance, finding that nationality hardly influenced attitudes; instead, dimensions of the Almere model (perceived enjoyment, sociability, usefulness, trustworthiness) were the strongest predictors. Participants viewed SARs as Assistive Technological Devices and expected public provision to follow national ATD practices. This study will serve as the primary internal validation dataset: simulated Italian agent distributions on the Almere trustworthiness and sociability dimensions will be compared with the empirical distributions reported by Fracasso et al. The finding that nationality was not a significant predictor is a useful null result that underscores the importance of individual‑level parameterisation.

A 2026 cross‑cultural study (Germany vs. Japan) on autonomous cleaning robots in public spaces found that Germans showed significantly higher existence acceptance than Japanese, with Social Norms and Trust as the strongest positive predictors across cultures. Germans followed a functional‑affective pattern; Japanese a trust‑emotion pattern. This study provides a validated methodology for city‑level cultural comparison that informs the model’s between‑city analysis.

