7. Execution and Data Acquisition Plan

This chapter translates the methodological commitments into a risk‑mitigated execution roadmap, addressing the most critical bottlenecks.

7.1 Individual‑Level Data Access: ESS and WVS

The ESS Rounds 8–10 microdata for France and Italy are publicly accessible and will be downloaded immediately. The WVS Wave 7 USA data require a formal application and typically 2–3 months for approval. I have initiated contact with the secretariat and prepared institutional compliance documents. As a fallback, the General Social Survey (GSS) contains comparable value measures and is available without lengthy approval; the agent sampler is built as a plug‑in module to allow seamless switching. A synthetic‑data pipeline will be used for development and testing from day 1, so that real‑data acquisition delays do not impede code development.

7.2 Mesa 3 Scalability Benchmark

The simulation demands up to 10 000 agents × 3 650 ticks × 100 runs (≈ 3.65 billion agent‑steps). A systematic benchmark will be conducted using a minimal test model on a 16‑core HPC node. If a single 10‑year run exceeds 6 hours wall‑clock time, the simulation horizon will be reduced to 5 years (capturing the critical 2026–2031 policy window) and/or batch‑runner optimisations (PyPy, vectorised updates) will be applied. A technical note will document the benchmark, ensuring transparency.

7.3 Socratic Trigger Calibration

A pilot parameter sweep is performed over k ∈ {3, 5, 7, 14} days and θ_over ∈ {0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9}, using 1 000 Rome‑only agents over 5 years. The combination that yields (a) < 15 % of agents with dependency ratio > 0.9 at year 5, and (b) specificity > 80 % (false‑positive triggers minimised) is selected for all full runs. Elderly agents are given a 50 % higher k to reflect their error‑resilient trust patterns, based on the 2025 older‑adult study.

7.4 EU AI Act Timing Monte Carlo (Stress‑Test)

Given the ongoing trilogue negotiations, the certification date distribution is:

· August 2026 (original deadline) – probability 0.20
· December 2027 (Annex III deferral) – probability 0.70
· August 2028 (Annex I, if applicable) – folded into the 2027 scenario with a separate flag for embedded systems robots
· Never – probability 0.10

Each simulation run draws a date from this distribution, altering the timeline on which robots receive the eu_ai_certified flag. The mean citizen trust at years 5 and 10 is computed under each scenario; the “trust cliff” is defined as the difference between the August 2026 and December 2027 outcomes.

7.5 Validation Data Split Implementation

Three non‑overlapping partitions are strictly maintained:

· Calibration: EU SAR survey (n = 1 092) – used to fit initial agent trust/anxiety distributions.
· Internal validation: Fracasso et al. (2022) Italian subsample – completely held out; comparison on Almere dimensions.
· External validation: Held‑out 20 % random subsets of ESS/WVS respondents; any future survey data from real‑world robot deployments.

