8. Impact Acceleration and Publication Strategy

This chapter describes the measures that transform the PhD into a high‑visibility, policy‑relevant research programme.

8.1 Pre‑registration and Exact Statistical Tests

Before any hypothesis‑testing run, the full protocol will be pre‑registered on the Open Science Framework. The registry entry will include hypotheses H1–H5, outcome variables, the exact statistical models listed in Chapter 5, and a complete RMarkdown analysis script. This pre‑registration follows the template of Brauner et al. (2026) and ensures compliance with Nature Human Behaviour standards.

8.2 Counterfactual Policy Experiments

Three scenarios are simulated to quantify the value of regulation:

Scenario EU AI Act High‑Risk Obligations Italy Law 132 Purpose
Baseline Deferred to Dec 2027 / Aug 2028 (Monte Carlo) Active Oct 2025 Realistic baseline
No Regulation Never adopted Never enacted Pure market‑driven trust and labour outcomes
Italy Only Never adopted Active Domestic vs. supranational regulation effect

These runs directly answer the question: “What is the citizen trust and employment effect of the EU AI Act under the current deferral?”

8.3 Threat Model for Overtrust

For Hypothesis 4, the policy‑ready metric is the percentage of Rome agents aged 65+ whose dependency ratio exceeds 0.9 by year 10 in the absence of Socratic intervention. This will be visualised as a heatmap by age and Security value. The output will be submitted to JMIR Human Factors and communicated to the Italian Ministry of Health’s elderly‑care policy unit.

8.4 Robustness Check: Kalman‑Filter and TIP Alternatives

To address the “Why Beta?” question, a parallel trust model based on a Kalman filter (mean + variance) will be implemented. Additionally, the TIP indirect‑trust extension will be activated in a subset of runs. If H1, H3, and H4 hold under all distributional assumptions, the cultural effects are robust; divergences will be characterised as boundary conditions.

8.5 Publication Architecture (Three‑Paper Dissertation)

1. Paper 1 (Methods + H1–H2): “A Bayesian Agent‑Based Simulation of Cross‑Cultural Trust Dynamics toward Robots in Three Cities.” Target: Journal of Artificial Societies and Social Simulation (JASSS) or Computational Communication Research.
2. Paper 2 (Socratic Debiasing + H4): “Preventing Overtrust and Dependence in Elderly Human–Robot Interaction: A Socratic‑Beta Computational Model.” Target: JMIR Human Factors or Science Robotics.
3. Paper 3 (Labour + H5 + Counterfactual Policy): “Employment and Autonomy Effects of Urban Robotisation: An Agent‑Based Counterfactual Across Paris, New York, and Rome.” Target: Computational Economics or Science Advances.

