9. Model Formalisation and Implementation Details

This section provides the complete mathematical specification of all agent‑level mechanisms, ensuring full transparency and reproducibility. The model is a discrete‑time stochastic ABM with tick granularity of one day. The full source code, parameter files, and experiment scripts will be released under an MIT licence.

9.1 Agent State Vector

Each human agent i at time t is defined by the tuple:

S_{i,t} = \langle \text{age}_i, \text{edu}_i, \text{sector}_i, \mathbf{v}_i, \text{anx}_{i,t}, \{\alpha_{i,r,t}, \beta_{i,r,t}\}_{r \in R}, \text{aut}_{i,t}, \text{dep}_{i,t}, \text{msens}_i, \text{inoc}_{i,t}, \text{emp}_{i,t} \rangle

where:

· v_i is a 10‑dimensional Schwartz value vector, normalised to [0,1] per dimension, centred on the individual’s ipsatised mean.
· anx_i,t ∈ [0,1] is the current anxiety level, initialised from the RAS distribution and modified by interactions.
· α_i,r,t, β_i,r,t are the Beta trust parameters for robot type r (logistics, social, domestic, healthcare).
· aut_i,t ∈ [0,100] is perceived autonomy (percent).
· dep_i,t ∈ [0,1] is the dependency ratio, defined as the proportion of daily decisions delegated to robots over the preceding 30 days.
· msens_i ∼ N(μ_ms, σ²_ms) is the individual media‑sensitivity coefficient, drawn from a distribution calibrated to match the effect‑size heterogeneity in Brauner et al. (2026).
· inoc_i,t ∈ {0,1} is the Socratic inoculation flag.
· emp_i,t ∈ {employed, unemployed, separated} is employment status.

Each robot agent j is defined by its type r_j, autonomy level a_j ∈ [0,1], error‑acknowledgment probability e_j ∼ Beta(a_e, b_e), true reliability ρ_j ∈ [0.7, 0.99], and EU AI Act certification status c_j,t ∈ {0,1} (time‑varying).

9.2 Interaction Protocol and Trust Update

On each day, each employed agent interacts with n_int robots drawn from their sector. Each interaction consists of a binary task (success/failure) drawn from Bernoulli(ρ_j). The trust weight w is computed as:

w_{i,r,t} = w_0 \cdot (1 + \lambda_{\text{err}} \cdot e_{j}) \cdot (1 + \lambda_{\text{reg}} \cdot c_{j,t}) \cdot \exp(-\gamma \cdot \text{anx}_{i,t} \cdot (1 + \text{Security}_i)) \cdot (1 + \delta_{i,r} \cdot \mathbb{I}_{\text{bias}})

where:

· Security_i is the agent’s Schwartz Security value (centred).
· γ = 0.8 (anxiety discount steepness).
· δ_i,r = 0.2 if age_i ≥ 65 and robot type is social or healthcare, else 0.05.
· The automation‑bias indicator 𝕀_bias is drawn from Bernoulli(p_bias) with p_bias = 0.15 for elderly agents interacting with social/healthcare robots, and 0.02 otherwise. If triggered and the outcome is a failure, α is incremented by w instead of β (self‑blame misattribution).

Trust parameters update:

\alpha_{i,r,t+1} = \alpha_{i,r,t} + w_{i,r,t} \cdot O_t\\
\beta_{i,r,t+1} = \beta_{i,r,t} + w_{i,r,t} \cdot (1 - O_t)

with O_t = 1 for success, 0 for failure (except under automation bias where a failure may be coded as O_t = 1). Mean trust for robot type r is T̄_i,r,t = α/(α+β).

9.3 Anxiety Dynamics

Anxiety is updated daily:

\text{anx}_{i,t+1} = \text{clamp}\Big( \text{anx}_{i,t} + \eta \cdot f_{\text{exp},t} \cdot (1 + 0.5 \cdot \text{Security}_i) - \zeta \cdot F_{i,t} - \mu \cdot \bar{c}_{i,t}, \ 0, \ 1 \Big)

where:

· f_exp,t ∈ {0,1,2} is the number of robot interactions that day.
· F_i,t = 1 - exp(-κ · N_success,i,t) is a familiarity logistic with κ = 0.05, and N_success the cumulative successful interactions.
· c̄_i,t ∈ {0,1} is the proportion of interacting robots that are EU‑certified that day.
· η = 0.02, ζ = 0.03, μ = 0.04.

9.4 Autonomy Degradation

Perceived autonomy declines as robot density in the agent’s sector d_sector,t (robots per 1 000 workers) increases:

\text{aut}_{i,t} = 100 \cdot \exp\left( - \phi \cdot \frac{d_{\text{sector},t}}{d_{\text{baseline}}} \right) \cdot (1 + 0.5 \cdot \bar{c}_{i,t})

where φ = 0.01, d_baseline is the initial density, and the certification buffer halfs the degradation rate.

9.5 Socratic Intervention Mechanism

The trigger condition is evaluated each tick for agents aged ≥ 60:

\text{trigger}_{i,t} = \mathbb{I}\left( \max_{r} \bar{T}_{i,r,t} > \theta_{\text{over}} \ \land \ \text{dep}_{i,t} > 0.7 \ \text{for} \ k \ \text{consecutive days} \ \land \ \text{inoc}_{i,t}=0 \right)

When triggered:

· α_i,r,t reset to 1, β_i,r,t reset to 5 for all robot types (skeptical prior, mean trust ≈0.167).
· inoc_i,t set to 1 for 365 days.
· δ_i,r halved (δ ← δ/2) permanently.
· dep_i,t is reduced by 0.3 (immediate behavioural effect) and then follows the standard update thereafter.

9.6 Media Shock Implementation

At the start of each month, a city‑level media event is drawn: positive (prob = 0.4), negative (0.3), or none (0.3). The shock magnitude for each agent is:

\Delta \text{benefit}_i = \text{msens}_i \cdot \beta_{\text{pos,benefit}} \quad \text{or} \quad \Delta \text{risk}_i = \text{msens}_i \cdot \beta_{\text{neg,risk}}

with β values taken from Brauner et al. (2026): β_pos,benefit = 0.52; β_neg,risk = -0.69. The shock decays exponentially with half‑life 15 days, modulating the weight w via:

w_{i,r,t} \leftarrow w_{i,r,t} \cdot \exp( \Delta_{i,\text{current}} )

where Δ_current is the residual shock on that day.

9.7 Labour‑Market Transitions

Each day, employed agents face a separation probability:

p_{\text{sep},i,t} = p_{\text{base, sector}} + \beta_{\text{robot}} \cdot \Delta d_{\text{sector},t} + \beta_{\text{EPL}} \cdot \text{EPL\_index}_{\text{city}} + \beta_{\text{age}} \cdot \text{age}_i

· β_robot = +0.02 for New York service sectors, −0.01 for Paris/Rome moderate‑labour‑cost sectors (complementarity).
· EPL_index_city: Rome 2.76 (OECD), Paris 2.82, New York 1.17.
· β_EPL = −0.005 (higher EPL reduces separations).
· p_base,sector calibrated from Eurostat / BLS separation rates.

Separated agents may become re‑employed after d days with probability proportional to a re‑employment rate that decreases with the duration of unemployment, creating hysteresis.

---

10. Sensitivity and Robustness Analysis Plan

A systematic multi‑layer robustness analysis will be conducted to quantify the stability of all claims.

10.1 Local Parameter Perturbation

Each free parameter (w_0, λ_err, λ_reg, γ, η, ζ, μ, κ, φ, β_robot, β_EPL, and media‑shock magnitudes) is perturbed by ±10 % and ±15 %. For each perturbation, the full simulation protocol (100 runs per city) is repeated, and the sign and significance of the primary hypothesis tests are recorded. A result is considered robust if the estimated coefficient retains the same sign and p<0.05 across all perturbations.

10.2 Alternative Trust Architectures

Two alternative trust updating rules are implemented:

1. Kalman‑filter trust: Trust is modelled as a Gaussian variable with mean μ_t and variance σ²_t, updated via standard Kalman equations with observation noise equal to 1/ρ_j. The same cultural and regulatory modifiers are applied to the update gain.
2. TIP indirect‑trust extension: The Trustworthy Influence Protocol (TIP) trust propagation is activated for agents who share the same district; indirect trust from neighbours modifies the prior. This tests whether social network effects alter the cultural moderation patterns.

If H1, H3, and H4 replicate under both alternatives, the cultural effects are robust to distributional assumptions; divergences will be characterised as boundary conditions and published as a secondary methodological contribution.

10.3 Structural Sensitivity

Three structural variations are tested:

· Alternative city cultural profile assignments: The Schwartz distributions are swapped between Paris and Rome to test whether the effects are driven by specific city economic structures rather than the value distributions themselves.
· Agent‑population scaling: Run the model at 1 000, 5 000, and 20 000 agents to check for aggregation bias.
· Media‑event frequency sweep: Probabilities varied from 0.1 to 0.5 to test the sensitivity of the population‑level perception volatility.

10.4 Policy‑Date Sensitivity

The EU AI Act Monte Carlo probabilities are varied systematically: a full grid of (P_Aug2026, P_Dec2027, P_Never) in 0.1 increments, subject to sum=1. The “trust cliff” magnitude is computed for each cell, producing a response surface that quantifies the behavioural cost of regulatory delay as a function of subjective probability.

---

11. Limitations and Boundary Conditions

All computational models face inherent limitations; this section transparently delimits the scope of inferential claims.

1. Cultural operationalisation scope: The model uses only Schwartz values, capturing motivational goals but not communication style, face‑saving behaviour, or specific cultural narratives about technology. A future extension could incorporate Hall’s high‑/low‑context communication dimension to moderate error‑acknowledgment probabilities.
2. Simplified urban geography: The environment is a stylised grid; it does not capture the spatial segregation patterns that mediate real‑world robot deployment (e.g., robot density clustering in wealthy districts). Spatial agent‑based extensions with realistic GIS layers could address this in future work.
3. Robot agency: Robots are stochastic reliability sources with fixed type‑level parameters; they do not adapt to user behaviour, a limitation that omits the co‑adaptation dynamics observed in long‑term HRI. Although the inclusion of an error‑acknowledgment parameter partially captures robot social behaviour, a nested learning agent (robot belief updating) would be required for full co‑evolution.
4. Labour‑market simplifications: The economic layer uses a binary separation/re‑employment model without wage adjustment, vacancy posting, or firm‑side heterogeneity. This is appropriate for a primarily HRI‑focused model but means H5 should be interpreted as directional, not as a precise forecast of unemployment levels.
5. External validity timing: Held‑out ESS/WVS data provide cross‑sectional snapshots, not the longitudinal trajectories the model simulates. The ultimate external validation requires field data from multi‑year robot deployment studies that are only now commencing. The model’s predictive claims are therefore conditional on the assumption that the mechanisms identified are the dominant drivers over the medium term.
6. COVID‑19 and pandemic legacies: The model does not include a historic shock that may have permanently altered technology trust baselines in some populations. Agents are initialised from pre‑pandemic (ESS R8) and post‑pandemic (R10, WVS7) survey data; a separate sensitivity analysis will flag run‑period effects by comparing initialisations from different ESS rounds.
7. Political and institutional shocks beyond regulation: The model treats regulation as the sole institutional variable. Sudden political shifts (e.g., a reversal of the AI Act following a change in European Parliament composition) are not modelled, though the “Never” scenario in the Monte Carlo partially captures a collapse scenario.

---

12. Conclusion and Future Work

This dissertation constructs and validates a cross‑cultural, individual‑level agent‑based simulation of human–robot integration across three global cities. By grounding agents in Schwartz value microdata, embedding a validated Bayesian trust core, and integrating time‑varying regulatory signals, media framing, and a Socratic debiasing intervention, the model makes a novel computational contribution at the intersection of HRI, cultural psychology, and AI governance.

The five falsifiable hypotheses target specific mechanisms: the Security‑value gradient of trust stability (H1), the regulatory trust premium and its deferral‑induced fragility (H2), the buffering effect of human‑oversight obligations on autonomy degradation (H3), the efficacy of Socratic triggers in breaking elderly dependency lock‑in (H4), and the divergent city‑sector employment pathways moderated by labour‑protection institutions (H5). Together they constitute a multi‑level theory of how individual values, institutional design, and public discourse co‑produce the societal outcomes of urban robotisation.

The pre‑registered inferential protocol, three‑way validation split, and extensive sensitivity and robustness analyses ensure that the knowledge claims are transparently assessed and openly reproducible. The counterfactual policy experiments directly address the European AI Office’s need for behavioural evidence on the consequences of regulatory deferral, while the overturst heatmap for Rome’s elderly population provides a concrete risk metric for health‑policy planning.

Immediate extensions beyond the PhD include:

· Spatially explicit model: Integrating GIS data on robot deployment and demographic distributions to capture neighbourhood‑level effects.
· Co‑adaptive robot learning: Endowing robot agents with a simple reinforcement learning mechanism to simulate the co‑evolution of trust and reliability.
· Multi‑wave longitudinal input: Using the upcoming ESS Round 11 data, which includes a rotating module on digitalisation, to recalibrate and externally validate the model’s 5‑year predictions.
· Real‑time policy dashboard: Transforming the simulation into a live web application that policymakers can use to explore “what‑if” scenarios for robot deployment regulation, media communication strategies, and debiasing programme design.

The PhD ultimately argues that trust in robots is not a static cultural trait but a dynamic, individually heterogeneous, and institutionally mediated process. By computationally formalising this process, it provides a rigorous, empirically grounded foundation for the human‑centred governance of urban robotics.

